new_runtime:
A regex with named groups sorts every image into a channel and a set (one field across all channels). chanID is the channel; plateID, wellID, fieldID and timeID say where a set goes; any other named group only tells sets apart. Images assigned to a channel by selection keep that channel.
Examples:
spaCR's Yokogawa default: {cellvoyager}
well, field and channel: {typical}
names made by Consolidate folders: {consolidated}

How much memory must stay free for everything else on this machine. When free memory falls below it, spaCR drops what it is holding rather than competing for the last of it.

Suggested:
{levels}

Plate barcode linkage: {wells} well(s), {mismatches} mismatch(es).
Plate map: {map_path}
Mismatches: {mismatch_path}

The most spaCR will hold in caches at once. Over it, the least recently used goes first — the one least likely to be wanted next — until what remains fits.

Suggested:
{levels}

The names cannot be turned into Yokogawa names with the current regex: not every image maps to a unique set of plate, well and field plus a channel.

{why}

Change the regex?

{n} image(s) are not a single grey plane:
{names}

Convert them? RGB images become grey (the mean of their colours) and z-stacks become their maximum projection. The originals are kept in originals/ of the sorted folder.

